From d077be9cf712c54681d7a5c3535793da9ad732e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:53:44 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 37/48] super-amd: rebuild volumes onto spares and returning
 members

When a slot of a managed volume is out of service, mdmon gives md a disk
to recover it onto, as the AMD driver would take one:

- the member's own disk, when it is back (it was missing, or stale): it
  is rebuilt in place, in its extent, as IMSM takes back a returning
  member; a disk failed on errors waits for the user, as with the AMD
  driver, and so does one whose extent another volume holds as well
  (the AMD menu and driver give the extent of an offline member to a
  new volume, clearing the old element; a mix of configurations of
  different generations can still have both);
- else an AMD global hot spare (SpareInfo 0x100), the first free extent
  as large as theirs.  The AMD driver holds a global spare to the
  members' controller only on a platform without arrays across
  controllers, and no platform we have seen is one: any controller will
  do.

The rebuild is recorded the way the AMD driver records it: the transform
log first, then RESTORE_SEGMENTS and the LD it rebuilds into; its
progress goes to the log, and its end puts the spare in the slot.  A
rebuild recorded so - by mdmon or by the AMD driver - goes on after a
reboot from where its log says, exactly where md stopped, when its
target is there as the volume starts.  A target that comes only after
the volume has started is copied from the start, as md may have written
without it (the AMD driver too copies from the start a target md wrote
without).  A former member whose PD record the AMD driver dropped gets
one again.  An mdmon that takes a volume over (--takeover, a restart)
records a rebuild md runs that was not recorded yet, and the end of one
md finished while no mdmon watched.

A disk that missed configurations and gets the next one all the same -
md recovers onto it, or rebuilds it in place - is up to date from then
on, to mdmon and to the AMD driver alike.  So an element of a mirror or
a RAID5 on it that md does not hold in sync goes offline first, and md
rebuilds it in place from 0: else the next assembly would take it as in
sync.  A target that lost the end of a rebuild by the AMD Windows driver
(a write cache lost at a power cut, in a VM) came back so, and md ran
the RAID10 without a rebuild, its copies apart.  An element offline on
a disk does not keep the disk from being the target of a rebuild
recorded onto it.

Members the AMD driver recorded missing (0x20, nothing written since)
go back in service without a rebuild when their disks come back with
the packet of the others - together, or one at a time (-I per disk, a
disk plugged in): the packet a disk came back with is its own, or, once
it has been given the live one here, the one its ring has before the
configurations it missed - their elements in service there at the same
places, and the members up to date cannot run the volume.  The AMD
driver takes them back so (seen with rcraid 9.3.3 in a QEMU guest: a
RAID1 recorded OFFLINE while its two disks were away, NORMAL again, its
data as they were).  A disk taken back whose PD the AMD driver dropped
gets its record again as its own packet has it, and its elements the
Handle.  The writes that can wait (the start and end of a rebuild, the
end of an initialization) wait only for disks still on their way, not
for one that is here with an older configuration: that one gets the
next configuration only as a rebuild target or a member rebuilt in
place, and would hold the end of a rebuild back for good.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
